{"name":"Frills","hex":"#8fa6c1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frills","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of frill"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frills"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ruffle (sewing)","blocks":[{"text":"In sewing and dressmaking, a ruffle, frill, or furbelow is a strip of fabric, lace or ribbon tightly gathered or pleated on one edge and applied to a garment, bedding, or other textile as a form of trimming."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Gullager_Salisbury.jpg/330px-Gullager_Salisbury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruffle_(sewing)","wikidata":"Q2204140"},{"title":"Neck frill","blocks":[{"text":"A neck frill is the relatively extensive margin seen on the back of the heads of reptiles with either a bony support such as those present on the skulls of dinosaurs of the suborder Marginocephalia or a cartilaginous one as in the frill-necked lizard."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Chlamydosaurus_kingii.jpg/330px-Chlamydosaurus_kingii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neck_frill","wikidata":"Q2917827"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/500px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/1280px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/500px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mechanical ruffler by Singer, used on domestic sewing machines